The Mesa Road
[trigger none]
[vignette dungeons/relay_post]

FIXME(mundane): The dust storm catches you on the mesa road. Visibility drops to nothing in a quarter mile. Grit stings exposed skin; the sky goes the color of rust. The scrub is too sparse to break the wind. You wrap cloth across your face and shoulder through it, half-blind.
# --- COLOR ---
# The dust storm reduces visibility to less than a quarter mile, with the mesa road disappearing into a wall of rust-colored particulate.
# Grit from the storm embeds in the seams of your clothing and stings exposed skin, especially around the eyes and mouth.
# The sky has taken on a uniformly rust-colored hue, with no discernible horizon or cloud structure.
# The wind carries a dry, mineral scent that coats the back of the throat and lingers on the tongue.
# The mesa road is composed of compacted red earth and fractured basalt, now nearly invisible under the storm's abrasive layer.
# Your cloth-wrapped face is damp with sweat and dust, and the edges of the cloth are stiff with accumulated grit.
# --- end ---
# --- FACTUAL ---
# The dust storm reduces visibility to less than a quarter mile, the mesa road swallowed by a wall of rust-colored particulate that blurs the edge of the world. Grit from the storm embeds in the seams of your clothing and stings exposed skin, especially around the eyes and mouth. The sky has taken on a uniformly rust-colored hue, with no discernible horizon or cloud structure. The wind carries a dry, mineral scent that coats the back of the throat and lingers on the tongue. The mesa road, composed of compacted red earth and fractured basalt, is now nearly invisible under the storm's abrasive layer. Your cloth-wrapped face is damp with sweat and dust, and the edges of the cloth are stiff with accumulated grit.
# --- end ---
# --- VOICED HPL mundane ---
# The dust storm had descended with a suddenness that seemed almost deliberate, reducing visibility to less than a quarter mile and swallowing the mesa road in a churning, rust-colored shroud that blurred the very edge of the world. The air was thick with particulate, fine as powdered bone and sharp as flint, driven by a wind that whistled through the fissures of the land with a dry, mournful hiss. Grit bit into the seams of my garments, worked its way beneath my collar, and stung the tender flesh around my eyes and mouth, each breath a rasping intrusion. The sky—once a pale, indifferent blue—had become a uniform, oppressive hue, a vast, unbroken dome of rust that concealed the sun and obliterated all sense of horizon or cloud. The wind bore a scent of ancient stone and desiccated earth, a mineral tang that clung to the back of the throat and settled on the tongue like ash. The mesa road, once a familiar path of compacted red earth and fractured basalt, was now all but erased beneath the storm's relentless, abrasive blanket. My cloth-wrapped face, damp with sweat and dust, had grown stiff and heavy; the edges of the kerchief were crusted with grit, and each movement of my head brought a faint, gritty whisper, as though the very air itself were composed of microscopic fragments of forgotten time.
# --- end ---
# --- VOICED REH mundane ---
# The dust storm howled like a banshee from the void, blinding the world in a curtain of rust-colored grit that choked the sky and swallowed the mesa road whole. Visibility shrunk to less than a quarter mile, the edge of the world erased as if the earth had been scoured clean. The wind clawed at exposed skin, stinging eyes and lips with a thousand tiny needles, while the dust worked its way into every seam of clothing, grinding against the flesh like sandpaper. Above, the heavens were a seamless, blood-tinged dome—no sun, no cloud, no hint of sky beyond the suffocating haze. The air tasted of dry stone and old bones, thick on the tongue and clogging the throat with every breath. The road, once a firm trail of compacted red earth and shattered basalt, was now a ghost beneath the storm's abrasive shroud, buried under a layer of dust so fine it clung like a second skin. Your face, wrapped in cloth, was damp with sweat and grit, the fabric stiff and crusted, each movement a grating rasp against the skin.
# --- end ---
# --- CRITIC factual ---
# (no findings)
# --- end ---
